      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240101197-eng/sections/page-009.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/traceability",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Traceability"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "traceability"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "The ability to trace something. In some cases, it is interpreted as the ability to verify the history, location or application of an item by means of documented identification (14)."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "SC handbook glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240101197-eng/sections/page-009.md, p. 9, citing the WHO policy paper on traceability of medical products, ref. 14). Internal/substantive alternative in SC handbook Ch. 4 boxed definition (who-iris/library/9789240101197-eng/sections/page-054.md, p. 54, citing GS1): \"Traceability is the ability to trace the history, application or location of an object. In a supply chain context, it is the ability to see the movement of health products across the supply chain.\" Shared across dth-sc, dth-pc, and dth-sfp; both definitions are recorded as candidates and neither is chosen (smart-ra#133 S1, smart-ra#140).",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240101197-eng/sections/page-009.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/trade-item",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Trade Item"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "trade item",
        "Trade item"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "Trade items are manufacturer-specific branded health products that are identifiable and unique to a particular brand or manufacturer."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "Owner decision 2026-10-31 (smart-ra#142 item 1, smart-ra#139): Trade Item is the shared WHO concept with this narrow definition from the Product Catalogue handbook Ch. 2 body (who-iris/library/9789240116191-eng/sections/page-028.md, p. 28). The GS1 trade item is a separate external concept and the WHO term's broadMatch (every WHO Trade Item is a GS1 trade item, not the reverse: GS1's also covers services). The PC handbook glossary (who-iris/library/9789240116191-eng/sections/page-009.md, p. 9) and SFP handbook glossary (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, page-011.md, p. 11) print the GS1 wording — \"Products or services that are priced, ordered or invoiced at any point in the supply chain\" — citing GS1 (PC ref. 13, SFP ref. 19); that sentence is recorded here as a note citing GS1, not as the WHO concept's definition.",
      "skos:related": [
        {
          "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/generic-product"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/product-master-data"
        }
      ],
      "skos:broadMatch": [
        {
          "@id": "https://www.gs1.org/standards/id-keys/gtin"
        }
      ],
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240116191-eng/sections/page-028.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/use-case",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Use Case"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "use case"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "A use case is an example scenario of how the end-user will use the system."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "PHC handbook glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, p. 11). Wording alternative in SC handbook glossary (who-iris/library/9789240101197-eng/sections/page-009.md, p. 9): \"An example scenario of how the end user will use a system.\" Both are recorded as candidates; neither is chosen (smart-ra#133 S1).",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/user-acceptance-testing",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "User Acceptance Testing"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "UAT",
        "User acceptance testing",
        "User acceptance testing (UAT)"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "User acceptance testing (UAT) is conducted to see whether the end-users are satisfied with the end product (24)."
        }
      ],
      "skos:notation": "UAT",
      "skos:scopeNote": "PHC handbook glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, p. 11). Wording alternatives across the DTH corpus (smart-ra#133 S1): (1) PC handbook glossary ('User acceptance testing (UAT)', who-iris/library/9789240116191-eng/sections/page-009.md, p. 9): \"UAT is done to see whether the end-users are satisfied with the end product.14\" (2) SFP handbook glossary ('User acceptance testing (UAT)', who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/page-011.md, p. 11): \"User acceptance testing is done to see whether the end users are satisfied with the end product (20).\" All three are recorded as candidates; none is chosen.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/user-persona",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "User Persona"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "Persona",
        "persona",
        "user persona"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "A generic or archetypical profile of an end-user that is representative of a single type of end-user."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "PHC handbook glossary wording under 'User persona', verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, p. 11). Internal and cross-DTH alternatives (smart-ra#133 S1): (1) PHC handbook §3.2.1 body (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-013-32-get-to-know-the-users.md, pp. 33-35): \"A user persona is a generic depiction of a relevant stakeholder, or end-user of the system who would be interacting with the PCPOSS.\" (2) SFP handbook glossary under 'Persona' (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/page-010.md, p. 10): \"A generic aggregate description of a person involved in or benefiting from a health programme.\" All three are recorded as candidates; none is chosen.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/user-story",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "User Story (Requirements)"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "User Story",
        "user story"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "Captures a long list of features from an end-user’s perspective. It describes who the end-user is, with their feature wants or needs, and the reason for that want or need (see section 3.2.3)."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "PHC handbook glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, p. 11). Internal and platform alternatives (smart-ra#133 S1): (1) PHC handbook §3.2.3 body (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-013-32-get-to-know-the-users.md, pp. 33-35): \"User stories are the start of generating a long list of requirements, in non-technical terms, that will help the technical team to determine the more specific functional requirements of the system.\" and the template \"“As a… [user persona], I want to… [do what I want to be able to do differently using an electronic system], so that… [reason for wanting that functionality].”\" (2) Platform glossary `user-story` (folio-assistant-core/glossary/cat-harness.glossary.json), which binds the story to a Role rather than a user persona: \"What a reader acting in a Role is trying to do, told as that role: as a <role>, I want <want>, so that <so that>.\" Also used as the functional requirements template (\"As a … / I want … / So that ...\") in SFP handbook §4.5 Table 12 (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-026-45-gather-requirements.md, page-043.md, p. 31).",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/workflow",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Workflow"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "workflow"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "The sequence of activities from one task to another (see section 3.3)."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "PHC handbook glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, p. 11). Internal and cross-DTH alternatives (smart-ra#133 S1): (1) PHC handbook §3.3 body (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-014-33-map-business-processes-and-workflows.md, pp. 35-39): \"Workflows are a visual representation of the progression of activities (tasks, events, interactions) performed within a business process (45).\" (2) SFP handbook glossary (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/page-011.md, p. 11, citing DIIG/DAK): \"A visual representation of the progression of activities (tasks, events, decision points) in a logical flow illustrating the interactions within the business process (21).\" (PHC glossary defines a workflow as the sequence of activities itself; PHC §3.3 and SFP glossary define it as the visual representation of that progression.) All are recorded as candidates; none is chosen.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/enabling-environment",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Enabling Environment"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "enabling environment"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "Attitudes, actions, policies and practices that stimulate and support effective and efficient functioning of organizations, individuals and programmes. The enabling environment includes legal, regulatory and policy frameworks and political, sociocultural, institutional and economic factors (3)."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "SFP handbook glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, page-009.md, p. 9, citing DIIG), matching DIIG Annex 1.1 Glossary (who-iris/library/9789240010567-eng/sections/page-139.md, p. 127). Shared DIIG Phase 1 concept across DTHs (smart-ra#133 S1, dth-sfp#1 §2.1 Cluster B1); recorded as a candidate.",
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/evaluation",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Evaluation"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "evaluation"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "The systematic and objective assessment of an ongoing or completed intervention with the aim of determining the fulfilment of objectives, efficiency, effectiveness, impact and sustainability (8)."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "SFP handbook glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, page-009.md, p. 9, citing Monitoring and evaluating digital health interventions, WHO 2016). Wording alternative in DIIG Annex 1.1 Glossary (who-iris/library/9789240010567-eng/sections/page-140.md, p. 128, citing WHO evaluation practice handbook, 2013): \"The systematic assessment of an ongoing or completed intervention to determine whether the intervention is fulfilling its objectives and to demonstrate an effect on health outcomes.\" Also contrasted with monitoring in SFP §6.1 (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-029-61-develop-a-monitoring-and-evaluation-plan.md, page-051.md, p. 39, quoting Pritchett et al. 2013): \"Evaluation asks whether the project is doing the right things, while monitoring asks whether the project is doing things right.\" Both definitions are recorded as candidates; neither is chosen (smart-ra#133 S1, dth-sfp#1 §2.1 Cluster B1).",
      "skos:related": [
        {
          "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/monitoring"
        }
      ],
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/health-information-system",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Health Information System"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "HIS",
        "health information system",
        "Health Information System (HIS)",
        "Health Information Systems"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "A system that integrates data collection, processing, reporting, and use of the information necessary for improving health service effectiveness and efficiency through better management at all levels of health services."
        }
      ],
      "skos:notation": "HIS",
      "skos:scopeNote": "DIIG Annex 1.1 Glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240010567-eng/sections/page-140.md, p. 128, citing WHO 2004 Developing health management information systems; also abbreviated in DIIG page-011.md and HRH handbook who-iris/library/9789240062627-eng/sections/page-006.md). The PHC handbook (who-iris/library/9789240093362-eng) has no HIS abbreviation or definition; a wording earlier attributed to it here is not in its text and was removed (smart-ra#200). Referenced in SFP handbook glossary under SMART Guidelines (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, page-011.md, p. 11) and Ch. 4 (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-021-4-determine-the-requirements.md, page-035.md, p. 23). The definition is recorded as a candidate (smart-ra#133 S1, dth-sfp#1 P2).",
      "skos:related": [
        {
          "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/health-digital-public-infrastructure"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/health-information-exchange"
        }
      ],
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240010567-eng/sections/page-140.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/monitoring",
      "@type": "skos:Concept",
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra"
      },
      "skos:prefLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Monitoring"
        }
      ],
      "skos:altLabel": [
        "monitoring"
      ],
      "skos:definition": [
        {
          "@value": "The continuous process of collecting and analysing data to compare how well an intervention is being implemented against expected results (8)."
        }
      ],
      "skos:scopeNote": "SFP handbook glossary wording, verbatim (who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md, page-010.md, p. 10, citing Monitoring and evaluating digital health interventions, WHO 2016). Wording alternative in DIIG Annex 1.1 Glossary (who-iris/library/9789240010567-eng/sections/page-141.md, p. 129, citing WHO evaluation practice handbook, 2013): \"The process of collecting and analysing routinely collected data to compare how well an intervention is being implemented against expected results and measure changes in performance over time.\" Both definitions are recorded as candidates; neither is chosen (smart-ra#133 S1, dth-sfp#1 §2.1 Cluster B1).",
      "skos:related": [
        {
          "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/smart-ra/v0/ns/glossary/dpi-h-ra/evaluation"
        }
      ],
      "dcterms:source": "who-iris/library/9789240120747-eng/sections/sec-002-glossary.md",
      "skos:note": "candidate"
    }
  ]
}
